# Deployment-specific settings for bridge.py, sourced by
# bridge-supervisor.sh. Copy to `bridge.env` (untracked) and edit:
#
#     cp remote/bridge.env.example remote/bridge.env
#
# Real paths, project codes and usernames go in `bridge.env`, never here
# and never in any other tracked file -- see CONTRIBUTING.md's "Sensitive
# paths". `bridge.env` is gitignored for that reason.

# Which herdr agent a request targets when it doesn't name one itself.
# Leave this unset in most deployments: the bridge then picks an agent
# that can take work, which is what you want and needs no maintenance.
#
# Naming agents is optional, and pinning this to a name ages badly --
# herdr attaches a name to an agent *session*, so it is dropped whenever
# the agent process restarts, not just when a window is rebuilt. A
# configured name that stops resolving takes every default request down
# with it. If you must pin, prefer a runtime family ("opencode",
# "claude"): it survives restarts and pane renumbering.
# SENTINEL_AGENT="opencode"

# Optional ordered allowlist for quota failover. When the active agent reports
# a provider quota/balance failure, bridge tries these existing Herdr agent
# names instead. Keep each fallback on a genuinely independent account or
# provider; the automatic discovery path otherwise only switches runtime
# families (for example, Claude <-> OpenCode).
# SENTINEL_QUOTA_FAILOVER_AGENTS="your-claude-agent,your-opencode-agent"

# Ordered cost preference, cheapest first. Matched against an agent's name
# or its runtime family, so "claude,opencode" keeps working after herdr
# drops a name. Unlisted agents sort after listed ones. Unset keeps the
# old behaviour, where the cheapest agent won only by coincidence of sort
# order.
#
# The bridge cannot work this order out for itself: whether a backend is a
# prepaid subscription or metered per token is a billing arrangement, not
# something herdr reports. A subscription's quota is already paid for
# whether it is used or not, so it usually belongs first.
#
# This only orders agents that can take work right now. A busy cheap agent
# loses to an idle expensive one, because there is no queue-and-wait path
# and preferring the busy one would just fail the request.
#
# Metered providers do vary price by hour -- DeepSeek bills peak
# 01:00-04:00 and 06:00-10:00 UTC on weekdays at double its off-peak rate
# as of 2026-09 -- but there is deliberately no schedule here: it would go
# stale silently, and it changes no decision while a subscription outranks
# the metered backend at every hour of the day.
# SENTINEL_AGENT_PRIORITY="claude,opencode"

# How long a quota circuit stays open before it expires by itself. Provider
# limits reset on their own -- the error text usually says when -- so a
# circuit that only an operator can clear leaves a recovered agent idle and
# unusable until somebody notices. Keep this shorter rather than longer:
# expiring early costs one wasted prompt and re-opens the circuit, expiring
# late costs the agent. 0 restores the old clear-by-hand behaviour.
# SENTINEL_QUOTA_BLOCK_TTL_SECONDS="3600"

# Where agents drop their result files and the bridge picks them up. Must be
# writable by both. Point this at a directory *under the agents' own working
# directory* -- `herdr agent list` (or GET /agents) reports each agent's cwd.
# The default lands in the system temp dir, outside that cwd, so every agent
# permission system (OpenCode's external_directory rules, Claude Code's
# auto-mode classifier) sees an external write and can stall the task after
# the work is already done. Under the agents' cwd it is an ordinary
# in-project write and the question never comes up.
# SENTINEL_RESULT_DIR="/path/to/the/agents/cwd/sentinel-bridge-results"

# How many days an uncollected result file survives before the sweep that
# runs at every bridge start deletes it. A collected file is removed the
# moment it is read, so only failed deliveries -- timeouts, orphaned tasks
# -- ever get this old. Set to 0 to keep everything (and clean up by hand).
# SENTINEL_RESULT_RETENTION_DAYS="7"

# Shared secret for the bridge's optional auth. ASCII only -- a non-ASCII
# value makes every authenticated request fail closed with 401.
# SENTINEL_BRIDGE_TOKEN="a-random-ascii-string"
